   {
    "id": "C10",
    "summary": "Hon Hai (Foxconn), the Taiwanese assembler of Nvidia's servers, reported August 2026 revenue of NT$921.8 billion (about $29.1 billion), up 52% year on year on AI server demand and well ahead of analyst expectations for the quarter, according to Bloomberg News; July revenue grew 54% and analysts were looking for around 37% growth in the three months to September. The item notes that Hon Hai's monthly filing has become a check-in for the AI trade.",
    "passages": [
     "Hon Hai reported August revenue of NT$921.8B, about $29.1B, up 52% year on year on AI server demand, well ahead of analyst expectations for the quarter.",
     "Hon Hai reported an August revenue rise of 52% to NT$921.8B, about $29.1B, Bloomberg News reported on Saturday. July grew 54%, and analysts are looking for around 37% growth in the three months to September, so the company is running well ahead of them.",
     "The Taiwanese company assembles Nvidia's servers, and its monthly filing has become a check-in for the AI trade."
    ],
    "original_party": "Hon Hai Precision Industry (Foxconn)",
    "statement_date": null,
    "source": {
     "title": "Nvidia partner Hon Hai’s sales climb 52% as Europe lines up its own orders",
     "publisher": "thenextweb.com",
     "publication_date": "2026-09-05",
     "source_uri": "https://thenextweb.com/news/hon-hai-foxconn-august-sales-up-52-percent-ai-servers-bull-partnership-angers-pardubice-eu-ai-gigafactories",
     "text": "Hon Hai reported August revenue of NT$921.8B, about $29.1B, up 52% year on year on AI server demand, well ahead of analyst expectations for the quarter. In June it signed a EUR 120M manufacturing partnership with Bull, the French state-owned computing firm, splitting work between Pardubice and Angers.x\nHon Hai reported an August revenue rise of 52% to NT$921.8B, about $29.1B, Bloomberg News reported on Saturday. July grew 54%, and analysts are looking for around 37% growth in the three months to September, so the company is running well ahead of them.\nThe Taiwanese company assembles Nvidia’s servers, and its monthly filing has become a check-in for the AI trade. Concerns about overcapacity, rising debt and fierce competition have grown around it, and the revenue line keeps ignoring them.\nTNW reported after the second quarter that AI servers now bring in more than everything else Hon Hai makes combined.\nThe rest is largely Apple. Foxconn, as it is also known, remains the primary iPhone assembler on thinner margins, with plants in China and India, and Apple is spreading work towards Luxshare Precision.\nBloomberg Intelligence analysts Steven Tseng and Rebecca Wang note that the consumer electronics business dilutes the cloud momentum, leaving gross margin and forward price to earnings below the peer average.\nSo the order book keeps compounding while the market argues about what the spending eventually returns. Demand and confidence have come apart.\nTNW has followed that argument through Big Tech’s cash flow , and Chinese investors marked the trade down this week despite the strongest earnings season in five years.\nEurope is about to add to the same order book, and it has already picked its assembler.\nBull and Foxconn announced a manufacturing partnership on 1 June with an initial commitment above EUR 120M, according to Bull’s own statement .\nComponents and initial testing go to Foxconn’s plant in Pardubice, in the Czech Republic. Final assembly, integration and system validation happen at Bull’s factory in Angers.\nFoxconn’s head of AI and quantum, Jesse Chao, said the collaboration advances “ building sovereign AI infrastructure in Europe ” and supports a resilient supply chain.\nThe French state finished buying Bull from Atos for EUR 404M in April, two months before the Foxconn announcement, and presented the purchase as a sovereignty measure.\nThe Commission has committed EUR 20B to AI gigafactories , with 77 proposals across 16 member states and construction of the first planned for 2027. The racks will be assembled in France from parts made in Czechia by a Taiwanese company whose monthly revenue is $29B."
    }
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "case": "6",
  "incoming": {
   "summary": "Micron Technology's management team believes the memory chip market will remain supply-constrained beyond 2027, a view fool.com on 5 September 2026 said leaves Micron room to benefit from soaring memory chip prices.",
   "passages": [
    "Micron's management team believes the market will be supply-constrained beyond 2027, leaving plenty of time for Micron to benefit from soaring memory chip prices, and investors should reap the benefits of this growth over the next few years."
   ],
   "original_party": "Micron Technology management",
   "statement_date": null,
   "source": {
    "title": "3 Incredible High-Growth Stocks to Buy With $10,000 Right Now",
    "publisher": "fool.com",
    "publication_date": "2026-09-05",
    "source_uri": "https://www.fool.com/investing/2026/09/05/incredible-high-growth-stocks-buy-nvda-nbis-mu/",
    "text": "If you're looking to add some high-growth stocks to your portfolio, there is a trio of options I've been eying that look like an excellent addition. These are some of the fastest-growing stocks on the market, and their growth routes vary, which is important for portfolio diversification.\nThe three stocks I'm recommending are Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , Micron Technology ( MU +4.20% ) , and Nebius Group ( NBIS +1.39% ) . These three stocks are some of the fastest-growing stocks available, and all are great buys now.\nNvidia may be a bit of a surprise, considering it's the world's largest company by market cap. But it just grew revenue by 106% year over year in its previous quarter. That shows demand for its GPU computing products remains high, and that any company involved in AI is utilizing Nvidia's products.\nHowever, its growth isn't expected to slow down anytime soon. CFO Colette Kress dropped a bombshell during the Q2 earnings call that management expects 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year. While that's just guidance, Nvidia has proven quarter after quarter that it tends to exceed its internal projections.\nEven if Nvidia meets its 70% growth expectations, that's an incredible growth rate for the world's largest company. Despite this growth, Nvidia's stock valuation remains cheap, trading at an attractive 23.4 times forward earnings.\nThat's valuation is near an historical low for Nvidia's stock, and getting a company growing that fast at that valuation is rare. As a result, it makes for a strong stock to buy now.\n2. Micron Technology\nMicron manufactures memory chips, which go into semiconductor chipsets like Nvidia's GPUs. There are also other use cases for memory chips in a data center, and the high demand for these products has constrained supply. This has caused memory chip prices to skyrocket, and companies like Micron are practically the only ones benefiting. Micron investors should also profit from rising prices, and the company's growth is nothing short of incredible.\nMicron reports Q4 results on Sept. 30, and the average Wall Street analyst expects 348% revenue growth year over year. For fiscal year 2027 (ending August 2027), analysts expect an additional 85% growth. Those are some incredible growth rates, and could be low projections if the memory chip shortage worsens.\nMicron's management team believes the market will be supply-constrained beyond 2027, leaving plenty of time for Micron to benefit from soaring memory chip prices, and investors should reap the benefits of this growth over the next few years.\n3. Nebius Group\nNebius may be a bit more under the radar than the other two, but it's also the fastest-growing of the three. It's a neocloud computing business, which means it provides cloud computing services dedicated to AI. This is obviously a hot industry to be in right now, and Nebius' growth results showcase it.\nDuring Q2, its revenue skyrocketed 454% year over year. That was primarily due to more computing capacity coming online, as it has rapidly increased its contracted computing power. In August last year, it had around 1 gigawatt of power contracts. This year, that figure has risen to 5 gigawatts.\nNebius is building and renting data centers as quickly as it can get its hands on them, as there is more than enough demand to drive these activities. But Q2's growth wasn't the peak either.\nFor 2026, the analyst community expects 533% revenue growth. For 2027, it expects 257% growth. That easily places Nebius on top of the list of fastest-growing stocks, and with the stock price down around 30% from its all-time high, now could be the perfect buying opportunity.\nBy Keithen Drury – Sep 5, 2026 at 9:44AM EST"
   }
  },
